Z||000000||Tom88gt||07-06-2001||12:23 PM||||My muffler blew out last weekend.  I finnaly dropped the entire exhaust today.  BIG hole on the back side of the muffler.  Caused my plastic tool box and the trunk carpet to melt together.  <P>Anyway, now to look for a replacement muffler.  I've done lots of searches and the popular item seems to be the Flowmaster 40 series.  BUT, every reference I can find says the exhaust pipe is 2.5" to the muffler and 2" from the muffler.  I just measured all the pipes and they are all appear to be 2" OD.  And it was the origianl GM muffler (date stamped 5-88).<P>Now I can get a muffler with a 2.5" inlets and 2" outlets or even a 3"/2.5" muffler.  But the laws of physics can't be changed.  An exhaust system is only going to flow as good as it's smallest point will allow.  Anything bigger anywhere else in the system doesn't matter.  Is a 2"/2" flowmaster available?  A bigger size won't hurt but is harder to fit to the stock pipes.<P>I was going to reuse the stock head pipe but what is the ID of the Fiero Store replacement headpipe?  If it's bigger than 2" then a bigger muffler and exhaust pipes might be a better option.<P>Ideal exhaust diameter?  My PAW catalog offers a rough guidline.<P>150-200c.i. (Fiero 2.8 is 173c.i.)<BR>100 HP...2.00" single or dual pipes<BR>150 HP...2.25" single / 2.00" dual<BR>200 HP...2.50" single / 2.00" dual<P>Using that guidline, the stock 2.00" diameter pipe is too small for even the stock HP.  Obviously a 2.25 is the prefered size for stock and 2.50" is better for a modified engine.<P>Any idea as to the diameter of the stock Y pipe between the manifolds?  My guess is 2.00" but without removing the heat sheilding I can't tell.<P>Finally, the CAT.  It had a hole in the top and was completely empty.  Nothing left so it's either a straight pipe or cherry bomb.||||reg||11
